\details{
\code{mu} is the \strong{shape} and \code{sigma} the \strong{scale} of the Gamma decision time,
so the mean decision time is \code{mu * sigma} and the median reaction time is
\code{ndt} plus the Gamma median.

\code{ndt}, \code{poutlier} and \code{minrt} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds rather than as a fraction of the fastest
observed response, what the half Student-t outlier component is for, and why
\code{minrt} is a constant on the family rather than a \code{dpar}.

Note that the Gamma density is \strong{unbounded at \code{ndt}} whenever the shape
\code{mu < 1}, which makes the likelihood unbounded as \code{ndt} approaches the
fastest response. The outlier component cannot repair that, since it adds
density rather than capping it. \code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} nests this family at
\code{shape = sigma} and lets the data choose the shape instead of fixing it.

Do \strong{not} fit this with \code{init = 0}, for two reasons at once: it puts \code{ndt} at
\code{exp(0) = 1} second, above most sub-second responses, and the shape at
\code{softplus(0) = 0.69}, inside the unbounded region above. No single scalar
avoids both - \code{ndt = exp(c)} wants \code{c} near \code{-1.6} while
\code{shape = softplus(c)} wants \code{c} above \code{1.9}. Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}}, which sets
them separately:

